T:Gurkha sing-song
T:Gurkha song, danced, with drum
C:traditional (Gurkha, Nepal)
O:India, Dharmsala (2nd Goorkhas)
R:song
S:Fox Strangways, The Music of Hindostan (1914), Ex. 129, p. 57
N:From a sing-song of the men of the 2nd Goorkhas after a hockey match: the songs followed so fast that no words could be taken down; one or two men whirled in the middle of the crowd with bent knees and elbows and upturned palms, a couple of bars at a time danced crouching, and at the end of a chorus there was sometimes a grunted "Ha! Ha!". No tonic is marked and there is no signature; the scale is G A c d with the fourth G-c as backbone (Fox Strangways's typical Gurkha form, p. 70), so the drone is on G. The plate prints a drum line under the first staff marked "Right." (the hands), then a second staff headed "Solo." (with "Drum as before.") and "Chorus."; only the first two-bar phrase is transcribed here, the solo and chorus wait.
N:HOW READ (2026-11-09): the first staff cropped at 1200, 1600 and 2400 dpi (scratch crops w-ex129s-*, v129-*, n-ex129a-*, d-ex129a-*); the detector gave G, c, A, G in bar 1 and G, A, c in bar 2, the B and d of the dotted figures were read by eye; the rhythm (quarter, then sixteenth and dotted eighth three times; dotted eighth and sixteenth, sixteenth and dotted eighth, half) from the 1600-dpi crop; one pass.
N:UNCERTAIN: the B and d in bar 1; whether the three snapped figures of bar 1 are sixteenth-plus-dotted-eighth as written or the reverse; the half note at the end of bar 2.
